¶ of C, which is l\ (/.^:| &] ''cl-^ ; 3) the red (marked cA), the leading note (or major Third of the dominant h-y dj^ /j "a-^) of the relative mhior in the key of G. The situation ¶ of these digitals is such that the lowest red) digital gives the natural seventh of the white digital immediately adjoining (below in the figure, compare C: '^C, F:"F). The middle (or yellow) digital gives the natural Seventh of the black digital, the right-hand top corner of which touches its left-hand bottom corner (compare Z>j: ''D-^, printed d d^). The uppermost (or blue) digital gives a note which is a small Semitone (24 : 25 = 70 cents) sharper than the note of the white digital on the left (compare D: i5., ijl, marked D: rfijl), and a diatonic Semitone (15 : 16 or 112 cents) fiatter than the note of the black digital on the right (compare dA-.E-^, marked f/:|J! : e). The only digital placed out of ascending order from left to right is the yellow one, which should have come between the two white digitals for so and re, but has been displaced from motives of convenience.

¶ Poole, as Mr. Colin Brown afterwards, provided only for modulations from ¶ keys into the dominant major, snbdominant major, and relative minor. ¶ the modulation into the tonic minor, therefore, he had to flatten by a comma. ¶ C minor was considered to be the relative minor of F\f major instead of E'^\) ¶ And although, in deference to Mr. Listen and Gen. Perronet Thompson, ¶ also made a provision for temporarily introducing the tonic minor if desired, ¶ col. 4 of the Duodenarium {Silliman, vol. xliv. p. 18, art. 35), he did not ¶ it himself. ' In the theory I have advocated,' he says, ' the major keys are ¶ on the first series of sounds,' p. 474c, col. 5, 'and the minor keys on the ¶ of the major keys,' ibid. col. 6. 'That there must be such a relation and ¶ is inevitable.' But this does not exclude taking minor keys also upon notes ¶ col. 5 as a base, considered, if desired, as the Sixths of major keys on the notes ¶ col. 4 of the Duodenarium. Otherwise tonality is destroyed by constant shifts ¶ a comma severely felt on justly intoned instruments. ¶ Mr. Poole was also aware of the alteration by a skhisma, and of the consequent ¶ of the number of pipes. He also refers to the 53 division, but he does ¶ seem to adopt either, and is not distinct enough on these points for me to ¶ his conclusions with certainty. In the duodenary arrangement, I have by ¶ lines marked the places where the skhisma comes into play, and by affixing ¶ cents to each note have shewn how it acts.

¶ will be seen that Mr. Poole had 100 notes to the octave, of which 39 arose ¶ the harmonic Sevenths. If the skhisma were neglected there would remain ¶ 36 tertian and 20 septimal, or in all 56 tones to the octave. The duodenary ¶ has been taken from Mr. Poole's Enharmonic table {Silliman, xliv. ¶ 13), consisting of 19 lines similar to the 3 at the bottom of fig. 70. He adds ¶ following example of the fingering of chords upon his keyboard, the double ¶ indicating 'that the key is touched with one finger and immediately ¶ for another '. The duodenals and mark of the natural Seventh are accord¶ to my notation, sect. E. art. 26, p. 465c. The vipper figures refer to the JVotes ¶ follow.
